(The deterministic gate. A fuzzer whose output is not reproducible cannot
 produce a usable repro.sh, so determinism is a correctness property worth
 gating on — and it is itself fully deterministic. Generation only, no
 compiling, no running, so it stays fast enough for ./test.sh.

 Two claims, both pinned:
  - same seed + budget, generated twice, is byte-identical. Text equality in
    Vox compares bytes (confirmed, and it is the same comparison
    tests/040_gen.vox relies on for its `reproducible` line).
  - different seeds produce different source. A generator that returned a
    constant would pass the first claim trivially; this second claim is the
    vacuous-test trap this project has been bitten by, and is the whole
    point. Different seeds differ because the prelude functions f1/f2 draw a
    constant from the RNG, so even budget 0 differs by seed.

 If generation ever depends on something outside the seed, a `1` below
 flips to `0` and the .expected diff fails the gate.)

a list called 'the seeds' is [1, 42, 999].
a list called 'the budgets' is [1, 8, 20].

a number called 'same pairs' is 0.
a number called 'distinct pairs' is 0.

To 'source of' with a number called seed and a number called budget.
    'gen program' of seed and budget.
    a text called t is 'gen text'.
    Return a text, t.

(Generate the same seed+budget twice and assert byte-identical.)
To 'check same' with a number called seed and a number called budget.
    a text called first is 'source of' of seed and budget.
    a text called second is 'source of' of seed and budget.
    If first is equal to second then,
        Set 'same pairs' to 'same pairs' add 1.
    Print "same seed {seed} budget {budget}: {first is equal to second}".

(Generate two different seeds at the same budget and assert they differ.)
To 'check differ' with a number called sa and a number called sb and a number called budget.
    a text called first is 'source of' of sa and budget.
    a text called second is 'source of' of sb and budget.
    If first is not equal to second then,
        Set 'distinct pairs' to 'distinct pairs' add 1.
    Print "differ seed {sa} vs {sb} budget {budget}: {first is not equal to second}".

(One seed at a time; the inner budget sweep is a single-line For-each whose
 own body period closes it, so there is no nested-clause-close trap.)
To 'sweep seed' with a number called seed.
    For each b in 'the budgets', 'check same' of seed and b.

For each s in 'the seeds', 'sweep seed' of s.

'check differ' of 1 and 2 and 12.
'check differ' of 1 and 3 and 12.

Print "determinism: {'same pairs'} same-seed pairs byte-identical, {'distinct pairs'} distinct-seed pairs differ".